[Music]  time now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week antibiotic  resistance that's when drugs lose their  ability to stop an infection it's a  growing health problem in the US and  around the globe according to the  Centers for Disease Control and  estimated 2 million Americans deal with  antibiotic resistant infections every  year and 23,000 end up losing their  lives a new report from the CDC looked  specifically at unusual types of  antibiotic resistance here to talk about  that and more is CBS News medical  contributor dr. Tara  Andrew litera good morning good morning  Dana so they call this nightmare  bacteria what does that mean exactly it  doesn't sound good know that I know  these bacteria are predominantly  untreatable so resistant to most  antibiotics they tend to be uncommon and  they also can carry genes that can  spread resistance to other bacteria  they're a big problem for hospitals and  nursing facilities they have a very high  mortality rate and so in this study they  say they took about six thousand samples  25% of them carried those genes that  could be resistance genes that they  could pass on and interestingly when  they looked at asymptomatic contacts of  people who had had these infections 11%  of those also were carriers are  essentially colonized with these  superbugs so the CDC really said this  was way more than they expected in terms  of numbers and probably underestimates  the true number so it is pretty scary  the report highlights the CDC's  containment strategy what is it and is  it working so containment strategy not  really a new thing and essentially they  liken it to stopping a wildfire so the  idea is to catch these things when they  are a spark or a very small fire to  contain them to extinguish them before  they become a wildfire and it's very  difficult at that point and so it's  really five steps that they talk about  the first is rapid identification of  these infections and the mechanism that  makes them resistant going in and having  on-site infection control screening  those people around the infected person  to see who might be asymptomatic and  colonized and then basically continuing  to have a facilitated coordinated group  of people involving hospitals labs the  CDC all looking at this and staying on  top  it until they've stopped the spread one  of the issues is the antibiotics that  are prescribed also in the antibiotic  resistance so what role do doctors play  in all this in prescribing antibiotics  well we do play a big role and certainly  education is key and we know that still  today about 30% of antibiotic  prescriptions given out in offices and  ers are unnecessary of the 70% that are  necessary we can do a better job at  picking the right drug picking the  duration that we give that drug and the  dose and I think one of the things the  CDC mentioned in this report that's so  important for people to understand is  that these antibiotics they are what  they call a safety net for large groups  of people cancer patients patients in  the ICU surgical patients organ  transplant patients if they don't have  access to antibiotics because they are  have now resistance in these bacterias  this is a problem for those groups our  next topic three women all cancer  survivors are suing a Cleveland area  fertility clinic after the clinic  unintentionally destroyed more than  4,000 embryos and eggs  the lawsuit announced Monday is the  latest against the clinic Tara has been  closely following the story  losing my fertility was not an option  for me in an emotional press conference  the cancer survivors told similar and  painful stories I risked my life and  delayed my chemotherapy treatments  because him having a family was so  important to me Rachael Mel says when  she learned her eggs were gone it was  like a punch to the gut because of the  carelessness of uhf University Hospitals  I have now lost all hopes of ever having  biological children the new lawsuit  alleges University Hospitals did not  notify its clients or double check to  make sure it's local and remote  monitoring systems were functional  despite being aware of issues with the  liquid nitrogen storage tank in the  weeks preceding last month's malfunction  in a letter to patients last week  University Hospitals admitted the tank  in question needed preventive  maintenance prior to the March 3rd  incident the tanks that protect embryos  are often exempt from government  oversight but there are proposals in the  works for new regulations Ohio State  Senator Joe Schiavoni  is in the  early stages of crafting regulatory  legislation with fellow lawmakers if  something goes wrong with the chemical  makeup of this tank then there should be  a trigger  there should be alarm and there needs to  be somebody there that can remedy the  situation immediately but for some the  suggested regulations have come too late  you H must be held accountable for  shattering our dreams and for forever  altering the courses of each of our  lives University Hospitals responded to  Monday's press conference in a statement  saying we watched three women share  their stories of the loss they suffered  we are profoundly sorry for their loss  and are committed to helping make sure  this never happens again anywhere your  your heart just breaks for them what can  they do in the future to prevent this I  think the biggest thing is that it's  been really a Wild West and so the FDA  and states who need to step in and  provide regulation dr. taryn iruma thank  you

